The smash of two approximable maps relates a smash neighbourhood W to a smash neighbourhood W' when W' is the product of the two masters, or both neighbourhoods are proper products whose factors are related by the two maps. Intersecting on the right, a master absorbs the other neighbourhood, and two proper products of the same domain neighbourhood intersect factorwise by the two maps; the result is the smash master or a proper smash neighbourhood. For monotonicity, a smaller smash neighbourhood has product form and a larger one is the master or a proper product, after which the factor inclusions and the component maps give the enlarged relation.
